Part 3 — Retrieving Deleted Areas

Recommended API: Option A

GET /workspaces/{workspaceId}/areas/deleted

Reasoning: The normal viewport endpoint contract (GET /workspaces/{workspaceId}/areas?bbox=...) must remain unchanged — it is explicitly ACTIVE-only and driven by a mandatory bbox param. Option B (?status=DELETED) would require making bbox optional or branching the endpoint behavior, which changes the existing contract. Option A is a dedicated endpoint for a distinct access pattern (management, not viewport rendering).

Recommended response DTO: DeletedAreaResponse

record DeletedAreaResponse(
    UUID id,
    UUID workspaceId,
    String name,
    int currentVersion,
    Instant deletedAt
)

No geometry. The Deleted Areas panel renders a list, not polygons. Excluding geometry keeps the payload small and avoids loading irrelevant data. currentVersion is needed as the expectedVersion argument for both Restore and Permanent Delete.

How to determine deletedAt

The area table has no deleted_at column — only created_at. The deletion timestamp must come from the AreaVersion record. Every successful soft delete creates exactly one AreaVersion with changeType = DELETED (this is invariant from the service code). The correct query: for each DELETED area, find the AreaVersion with the highest version number (which will have change_type = DELETED since the area's current_version always points to the deletion entry). This can be implemented as a JOIN:

SELECT a.id, a.workspace_id, a.name, a.current_version, av.created_at AS deleted_at
FROM area a
JOIN area_version av ON av.area_id = a.id AND av.version = a.current_version
WHERE a.workspace_id = :workspaceId AND a.status = 'DELETED'
ORDER BY av.created_at DESC

This is a guaranteed single join because area_version(area_id, version) has a unique constraint uq_area_version. No new columns are needed.

