Part 4 — Restore

Backend flow: unchanged

POST /workspaces/{workspaceId}/areas/{areaId}/versions/{version}/restore already handles the full restore semantics. After a successful restore:
- Area status becomes ACTIVE
- A new AreaVersion with ChangeType.RESTORED is appended
- AREA_RESTORED event is broadcast via WebSocket
- All connected clients' handleRealtimeEvent() calls loadViewport(), which loads the area if it is now in the viewport

Target version for Deleted Areas panel restore

The frontend should restore to currentVersion - 1 (the last committed state before the deletion). This is the same logic as the existing restoreDeletedArea() method. It works for multiply-deleted areas too: if the lifecycle is v1=CREATED, v2=DELETED, v3=RESTORED, v4=DELETED, then currentVersion - 1 = 3 (the RESTORED ACTIVE version), which is correct.

Restored area outside viewport

If the restored area's geometry does not intersect the current viewport, loadViewport() simply won't include it in the response — the area is not rendered. This is correct behavior. The area is in the database as ACTIVE; it will appear the next time the user pans to its location.

After restore: Deleted Areas panel update

The panel should remove the restored area from its list immediately (client-side removal on success), without waiting for a WebSocket event. Other clients with the panel open will receive the AREA_RESTORED WebSocket event; they can reload the deleted list on AREA_RESTORED (the area is no longer DELETED).

