Part 7 — AreaVersion FK behavior

The DB schema in V2__core_schema.sql defines:

CONSTRAINT fk_area_version_area FOREIGN KEY (area_id) REFERENCES area (id)

No ON DELETE CASCADE clause. PostgreSQL default FK behavior without an explicit action is NO ACTION, which is functionally equivalent to RESTRICT — it rejects the parent deletion if child rows exist.

Answers:

1. Physical deletion of Area row today: Rejected with FK constraint violation if any area_version rows exist for that area.
2. ON DELETE CASCADE? No — not in the schema.
3. JPA cascade/orphanRemoval? None. Area has no @OneToMany collection of AreaVersion. AreaVersion.area is @ManyToOne with no cascade. JPA will not cascade anything.
4. Would AreaVersion rows survive? The deletion would be rejected before any rows are modified.
5. Would the FK reject the deletion? Yes.
6. Migration required? A migration is required — but not to add ON DELETE CASCADE. The preferred approach is to explicitly delete AreaVersion rows in the service before deleting the Area row. This is more readable and keeps the deletion semantics explicit in code. The existing schema does not need to change. No new migration is needed for the FK — the service will perform the two deletes in the correct order within a single transaction.

Implementation sequence in permanentDeleteArea():
// Within @Transactional
areaVersionRepository.deleteAllByAreaId(areaId);  // new repo method
areaRepository.deleteById(areaId);

This requires adding deleteAllByAreaId(UUID areaId) to AreaVersionRepository — a derived delete method that Spring Data resolves to DELETE FROM area_version WHERE area_id = :areaId.

