Part 10 — WebSocket behavior

Recommended approach: Broadcast AREA_PERMANENTLY_DELETED

When Alice permanently deletes Area X and Bob also has the Deleted Areas panel open, Bob should not continue showing Area X as restorable indefinitely. The cleanest solution is to broadcast a new AREA_PERMANENTLY_DELETED event after commit.

Event shape: AreaEventMessage("AREA_PERMANENTLY_DELETED", workspaceId, timestamp, payload) where payload is the area data captured before deletion (the area still exists at the point the service runs, so we can read it). The frontend only needs the id from the payload to remove the area from the deleted list.

Frontend handleRealtimeEvent() must be updated:
- For AREA_PERMANENTLY_DELETED: do NOT call loadViewport() (the area was not in the normal map state). Instead, if the Deleted Areas panel is loaded, remove the area with that ID from the list.
- For all other event types: existing behavior unchanged.

Why not reuse AREA_DELETED? AREA_DELETED currently triggers loadViewport() which correctly removes a just-soft-deleted area from the normal map. If AREA_PERMANENTLY_DELETED reused this type, clients would needlessly reload the viewport for an area that was already absent from the normal map.

AreaEventMessage record can be reused — the type field is a String, so adding "AREA_PERMANENTLY_DELETED" requires no Java model change. The AreaMetricsListener.mapToChangeType() default case returns null for unknown types, so the existing metrics listener will silently ignore it (which is correct — we track permanent deletions via a separate counter).

