Part 11 — Concurrency

Restore vs permanent delete (race)

- Alice: conditionalRestore() → status becomes ACTIVE, version incremented → commit
- Bob (permanent delete, arrives after Alice commits): finds area.status == ACTIVE → 409 AREA_NOT_DELETED
- Bob (permanent delete arrives before Alice commits): service checks status == DELETED → passes → acquires row lock via deleteById. Alice's conditionalRestore arrives → tries to update row → row is gone after Bob commits → areaRepository.findById(areaId) throws AreaNotFoundException → Bob's response 200, Alice's restore gets 404 AREA_NOT_FOUND (or rather AREA_VERSION_NOT_FOUND if the version lookup runs first). This edge case is acceptable — the later transaction loses.

In practice, both operations are short transactions and the status check + status precondition enforce correct behavior for the common cases.

Two simultaneous permanent deletes

Both read status == DELETED, proceed. First to commit: deletes AreaVersion rows, then Area row. Second: areaRepository.findById(areaId) finds nothing → throws AreaNotFoundException → 404 AREA_NOT_FOUND. One succeeds, one gets 404 — correct and acceptable.

