Explicit Answers

A. Can a normal user currently restore an Area after deleting it?

Only if they do so immediately, in the same browser session, without dismissing the "Area Deleted" panel. There is no way to find a deleted area after the panel is dismissed, after a page reload, or if another user performed the delete. Restore is functionally not reliably usable today.

B. How will a user find deleted Areas after Task 19?

Via a "Deleted Areas" button/panel on the map that fetches GET /workspaces/{workspaceId}/areas/deleted and shows a list of deleted areas with name, deletion date, and Restore / Delete permanently actions.

C. Is soft-deleted data automatically expired?

No. DELETED Areas are retained indefinitely. No scheduled cleanup job exists or will be added in this task.

D. What exactly does "Delete permanently" remove?

All area_version rows with area_id = :areaId, then the area row itself. No data for that area remains in the database.

E. Can an ACTIVE Area be permanently deleted directly?

No. Permanent delete checks area.status == DELETED and returns 409 AREA_NOT_DELETED if the area is ACTIVE. The two-step lifecycle (soft delete first, permanent delete second) is enforced.

F. Does permanent deletion preserve or destroy AreaVersion history?

Destroys it. All associated AreaVersion rows are explicitly deleted (via deleteAllByAreaId) before the Area row is deleted, within the same transaction.

G. Is PURGED a database status?

No. AreaStatus remains {ACTIVE, DELETED}. No PURGED status, no PURGED change_type. No schema migration is needed.

H. Is a scheduled cleanup job required?

No. The retention policy is: DELETED areas are retained indefinitely until the user explicitly chooses "Delete permanently". No scheduled job, no time-based expiry.

I. How are two clients kept consistent when one restores or permanently deletes an Area?

Restore: broadcasts existing AREA_RESTORED event → all clients call loadViewport() → area appears on map if in viewport; other clients with Deleted Areas panel open reload the list on AREA_RESTORED (area disappears from the list since it is now ACTIVE).

Permanent delete: broadcasts new AREA_PERMANENTLY_DELETED event → clients with Deleted Areas panel open remove the area from the list; normal map clients ignore the event (the area was not in their viewport state anyway).

J. Does Task 19 require a database migration?

No. The implementation uses the existing schema. No new columns, no FK changes, no CHECK constraint additions. The deletedAt timestamp is derived from the AreaVersion join. The permanent delete is handled in the service (delete children first, then parent) without requiring ON DELETE CASCADE.

K. Does Task 19 require any new dependency?

No. All capabilities (JPA, Micrometer, existing WebSocket infrastructure, Spring REST) are already present.

